WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.465
Lesson zero point seven.

00:00:02.465 --> 00:00:05.470
Your first DeFi steps, practice mode first.

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:07.765
By the end, you'll connect a wallet to

00:00:07.765 --> 00:00:10.826
an app, understand exactly what you're signing, and

00:00:10.826 --> 00:00:13.239
practise on a free test network before a

00:00:13.239 --> 00:00:15.005
single real dollar is at risk.

00:00:16.305 --> 00:00:18.410
Here's the shift this lesson makes.

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:20.123
Every lesson so far has been

00:00:20.123 --> 00:00:22.550
about exchanges and your own wallet.

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:24.990
From here, you start talking directly to

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:28.445
apps, DeFi protocols themselves, through your wallet.

00:00:28.445 --> 00:00:31.383
That's a genuinely new kind of interaction, and it

00:00:31.383 --> 00:00:34.090
comes with a genuinely new kind of prompt: the

00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:36.740
popup asking you to sign or approve something.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.330
Here's the plan.

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:41.908
First, the four actions a wallet popup can

00:00:41.908 --> 00:00:44.845
ask for, and which ones actually move money.

00:00:44.845 --> 00:00:46.647
Second, why you practise on a

00:00:46.647 --> 00:00:48.990
free testnet before anything real.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:51.282
Third, what an approval actually does under

00:00:51.282 --> 00:00:53.145
the hood, and how to limit it.

00:00:53.145 --> 00:00:55.308
And finally, a real worked example,

00:00:55.308 --> 00:00:56.870
your checklist and a quiz.

00:00:58.170 --> 00:00:59.240
The four actions.

00:01:00.540 --> 00:01:01.650
Here they are, in the

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.515
order you'll usually meet them.

00:01:03.515 --> 00:01:05.203
Connect wallet lets a site see

00:01:05.203 --> 00:01:07.355
your public address, nothing more.

00:01:07.355 --> 00:01:09.480
It cannot move funds by itself.

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:12.530
Sign a message proves you own that address, often

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:15.579
just to log in, though some 'messages' are really

00:01:15.579 --> 00:01:18.685
permissions in disguise, more on that shortly.

00:01:18.685 --> 00:01:21.124
Approve gives an app permission to move a

00:01:21.124 --> 00:01:23.630
specific token, up to a limit you set.

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:25.940
And transact is the one that actually

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:28.190
does something, a swap or a deposit,

00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:30.135
and it's the one that costs gas.

00:01:31.435 --> 00:01:32.865
Notice the split.

00:01:32.865 --> 00:01:35.694
Connecting and signing a plain message cannot move

00:01:35.694 --> 00:01:37.825
a single coin out of your wallet.

00:01:37.825 --> 00:01:39.960
They're low-risk, and usually free.

00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:42.895
Approving and transacting are the two that can,

00:01:42.895 --> 00:01:45.585
which is exactly why they deserve your full

00:01:45.585 --> 00:01:48.030
attention every single time they appear.

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:52.785
Here's roughly what each action costs, side by side.

00:01:52.785 --> 00:01:54.235
Connecting: free.

00:01:54.235 --> 00:01:56.525
Signing a login message: free.

00:01:56.525 --> 00:01:59.008
An approval or a swap: a small amount of

00:01:59.008 --> 00:02:02.580
gas, since it's writing something to the blockchain itself.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:06.805
One caveat, worth remembering from here on.

00:02:06.805 --> 00:02:08.798
Some sites dress up an approval as

00:02:08.798 --> 00:02:10.920
if it were a harmless signature.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.459
Reading the popup's actual wording, not just

00:02:13.459 --> 00:02:15.603
clicking through it, is the one habit

00:02:15.603 --> 00:02:17.860
that catches that difference every time.

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:20.635
Practice first, for free.

00:02:21.935 --> 00:02:24.284
Here's the tool that makes all of this free

00:02:24.284 --> 00:02:26.880
to practise, with nothing real on the line.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.027
A testnet is a practice version of a

00:02:29.027 --> 00:02:31.929
blockchain, running the same software as the real

00:02:31.929 --> 00:02:34.840
thing, but with coins that are worth nothing.

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:37.920
Ethereum's main testnet is called Sepolia.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.105
Mistakes there cost exactly nothing.

00:02:41.405 --> 00:02:43.400
Compare the two side by side.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:45.815
On a testnet, the coins are free from a

00:02:45.815 --> 00:02:49.315
faucet, a giveaway site, and a mistake costs nothing.

00:02:49.315 --> 00:02:51.879
On mainnet, the real network, coins cost

00:02:51.879 --> 00:02:54.305
real money, and a mistake can too.

00:02:54.305 --> 00:02:56.996
Practising on the left before ever touching the

00:02:56.996 --> 00:02:59.295
right is the entire point of this lesson.

00:03:00.595 --> 00:03:02.680
Here's the practice run, in order.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:05.108
Add the Sepolia test network in your wallet,

00:03:05.108 --> 00:03:07.620
most have a 'show test networks' setting.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.638
Get free test ETH from a Sepolia faucet,

00:03:10.638 --> 00:03:13.808
a giveaway site built for exactly this.

00:03:13.808 --> 00:03:15.883
Make a test swap on a testnet

00:03:15.883 --> 00:03:18.720
version of a well-known exchange app, or

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.303
simply send test ETH to yourself.

00:03:21.303 --> 00:03:23.975
Then look up that transaction on a block

00:03:23.975 --> 00:03:26.713
explorer, which Lesson 1.2 covers in full.

00:03:28.013 --> 00:03:30.349
If a faucet doesn't hand over test

00:03:30.349 --> 00:03:33.518
ETH right away, don't worry, that's common.

00:03:33.518 --> 00:03:35.800
Try a different Sepolia faucet if

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:38.718
one is rate-limited or temporarily out.

00:03:38.718 --> 00:03:41.906
Some require a small existing mainnet balance or

00:03:41.906 --> 00:03:45.133
a free sign-in, precisely to slow down abuse.

00:03:45.133 --> 00:03:46.811
And a minute or two of waiting

00:03:46.811 --> 00:03:48.923
before it arrives is completely normal.

00:03:50.223 --> 00:03:53.131
Even experienced DeFi users still reach for a

00:03:53.131 --> 00:03:56.544
testnet years in, whenever something is genuinely new

00:03:56.544 --> 00:03:59.136
to them: an unfamiliar chain, a new kind

00:03:59.136 --> 00:04:02.533
of contract, an app they've never touched before.

00:04:02.533 --> 00:04:04.768
It isn't a beginner-only habit.

00:04:04.768 --> 00:04:05.913
It's a permanent one.

00:04:07.213 --> 00:04:09.793
Only after that practice run, move to a

00:04:09.793 --> 00:04:12.803
Layer 2 with a genuinely tiny real amount.

00:04:12.803 --> 00:04:15.561
Connect to a well-known app, read every single

00:04:15.561 --> 00:04:18.319
prompt before approving it, do one small swap,

00:04:18.319 --> 00:04:21.083
then revoke the approval when you're done.

00:04:21.083 --> 00:04:23.934
That sequence, small and deliberate, is how

00:04:23.934 --> 00:04:26.785
every experienced DeFi user actually starts.

00:04:28.085 --> 00:04:29.760
How approvals actually work.

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:34.225
Here's what actually happens when you approve a token.

00:04:34.225 --> 00:04:36.130
You're not sending anything yet.

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:38.938
You're writing a permission into the token's own

00:04:38.938 --> 00:04:41.172
contract: this app may move up to this

00:04:41.172 --> 00:04:43.535
much of this token, from my address.

00:04:43.535 --> 00:04:46.457
The app only uses that permission the moment

00:04:46.457 --> 00:04:49.550
you separately confirm a swap or a deposit.

00:04:49.550 --> 00:04:51.895
Two different actions, two different moments.

00:04:53.195 --> 00:04:55.007
This is exactly why the amount

00:04:55.007 --> 00:04:57.060
in that popup matters so much.

00:04:57.060 --> 00:05:00.072
'Unlimited' means the app could move your entire

00:05:00.072 --> 00:05:02.470
balance of that token, today or at any

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:05.290
point in the future, until you revoke it.

00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:08.069
A limited approval, say, twenty dollars, caps

00:05:08.069 --> 00:05:10.607
the exposure at twenty dollars, no matter

00:05:10.607 --> 00:05:12.480
what happens to that app later.

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:16.624
Here's what that difference looks like on a

00:05:16.624 --> 00:05:19.857
real wallet holding, say, eight hundred and fifty

00:05:19.857 --> 00:05:23.195
dollars of U.S.D.C., purely as an illustration.

00:05:23.195 --> 00:05:25.395
An unlimited approval exposes the

00:05:25.395 --> 00:05:27.700
entire eight hundred and fifty.

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:31.663
A twenty-dollar approval exposes exactly twenty, regardless of

00:05:31.663 --> 00:05:33.801
what the rest of the wallet holds.

00:05:35.102 --> 00:05:37.361
Here's exactly how that risk shows up

00:05:37.361 --> 00:05:39.501
in the real world, so you recognise

00:05:39.501 --> 00:05:41.287
it by shape, not by name.

00:05:41.287 --> 00:05:43.454
A fake or copycat site asks you to

00:05:43.454 --> 00:05:46.798
'verify your wallet' or 'claim' something, dressed up

00:05:46.798 --> 00:05:49.327
as routine, sometimes as a giveaway.

00:05:49.327 --> 00:05:52.691
The popup underneath that friendly headline is really an

00:05:52.691 --> 00:05:56.592
unlimited approval, worded to look like a harmless signature.

00:05:56.592 --> 00:05:59.161
Approve without reading the actual wording, and

00:05:59.161 --> 00:06:01.602
the app now holds a standing permission.

00:06:01.602 --> 00:06:03.692
Funds can then move later, on the

00:06:03.692 --> 00:06:06.766
attacker's own schedule, sometimes days or months

00:06:06.766 --> 00:06:09.287
after you forgot the popup ever happened.

00:06:10.587 --> 00:06:12.560
In practice, here's what to actually

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:14.727
look at before confirming anything.

00:06:14.727 --> 00:06:17.422
Which token, and which amount, precisely.

00:06:17.422 --> 00:06:19.757
Does the underlying contract address match

00:06:19.757 --> 00:06:21.632
the site you think you're on.

00:06:21.632 --> 00:06:24.697
Does the word 'Unlimited' appear anywhere in that popup.

00:06:24.697 --> 00:06:27.336
And if anything about it feels off, close the

00:06:27.336 --> 00:06:30.146
popup and look up the app independently first; a

00:06:30.146 --> 00:06:33.072
legitimate one will still be there in five minutes.

00:06:34.372 --> 00:06:36.033
One nuance worth adding, so

00:06:36.033 --> 00:06:38.112
this doesn't read as absolute.

00:06:38.112 --> 00:06:40.432
This isn't an argument for editing every

00:06:40.432 --> 00:06:43.052
approval down to the exact cent, forever.

00:06:43.052 --> 00:06:45.219
A protocol you use daily and trust

00:06:45.219 --> 00:06:48.253
deeply might reasonably carry a sensible working

00:06:48.253 --> 00:06:51.772
limit, reviewed occasionally, rather than Unlimited.

00:06:51.772 --> 00:06:54.622
A brand-new or unfamiliar app gets the opposite

00:06:54.622 --> 00:06:58.067
treatment: the smallest amount that completes what you're

00:06:58.067 --> 00:07:00.561
doing, revoked the moment you're finished.

00:07:01.861 --> 00:07:04.086
And here's how to undo it, any time.

00:07:04.086 --> 00:07:06.786
Open a revocation tool such as revoke dot

00:07:06.786 --> 00:07:09.935
cash, connect the same wallet, find the approval

00:07:09.935 --> 00:07:12.421
you no longer need, and revoke it.

00:07:12.421 --> 00:07:14.470
It costs a small amount of gas, since

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:17.004
it's writing to the blockchain, and it's worth

00:07:17.004 --> 00:07:19.431
doing the moment you're finished with an app.

00:07:20.731 --> 00:07:23.421
One habit ties this whole section together.

00:07:23.421 --> 00:07:25.412
Limit the amount before you confirm,

00:07:25.412 --> 00:07:27.381
and revoke it once you're done.

00:07:27.381 --> 00:07:29.667
Do those two things, and an approval can

00:07:29.667 --> 00:07:32.176
never quietly outlive the reason you gave it.

00:07:33.476 --> 00:07:34.881
A real worked example.

00:07:36.181 --> 00:07:38.992
Priya connects her wallet to a decentralised

00:07:38.992 --> 00:07:41.291
exchange on Arbitrum, a Layer 2.

00:07:41.291 --> 00:07:45.548
The wallet pops up: 'Approve USDC: Unlimited.' She edits

00:07:45.548 --> 00:07:49.656
that amount down to $20 before confirming anything.

00:07:49.656 --> 00:07:52.429
She swaps $20 of USDC for

00:07:52.429 --> 00:07:56.036
ETH, paying about $0.05 in gas.

00:07:56.036 --> 00:07:58.820
She checks the transaction on a block explorer

00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:01.726
to confirm it did exactly what she expected.

00:08:01.726 --> 00:08:03.910
Then she revokes the approval, since she's

00:08:03.910 --> 00:08:05.281
done with that app for now.

00:08:06.581 --> 00:08:08.545
Notice what made that safe wasn't

00:08:08.545 --> 00:08:10.636
luck, or any special knowledge.

00:08:10.636 --> 00:08:13.335
It was five small, repeatable habits, applied

00:08:13.335 --> 00:08:15.448
in order, on an amount small enough

00:08:15.448 --> 00:08:18.271
that even a mistake wouldn't have mattered.

00:08:18.271 --> 00:08:20.627
That's the whole template for every DeFi

00:08:20.627 --> 00:08:22.926
interaction that follows in this course.

00:08:24.226 --> 00:08:25.406
Checklist and quiz.

00:08:26.706 --> 00:08:28.601
Here's this lesson's checklist.

00:08:28.601 --> 00:08:32.691
Know the difference between connect, sign, approve and transact.

00:08:32.691 --> 00:08:35.251
Practise on the Sepolia testnet first.

00:08:35.251 --> 00:08:37.063
Make one small real swap on a

00:08:37.063 --> 00:08:39.356
Layer 2, with a limited approval.

00:08:39.356 --> 00:08:42.151
Find that transaction on a block explorer.

00:08:42.151 --> 00:08:44.426
And revoke the approval once you're done with it.

00:08:45.726 --> 00:08:46.936
Question one.

00:08:46.936 --> 00:08:49.386
Can 'connect wallet' move your funds by itself?

00:08:53.806 --> 00:08:56.351
The answer: no. It shares your address.

00:08:56.351 --> 00:08:57.735
Only an approval or a

00:08:57.735 --> 00:08:59.999
transaction can actually move funds.

00:09:01.299 --> 00:09:02.544
Question two.

00:09:02.544 --> 00:09:04.209
What's a testnet actually for?

00:09:08.629 --> 00:09:11.741
The answer: practising with worthless coins, so a mistake

00:09:11.741 --> 00:09:14.639
costs nothing while you're still learning the buttons.

00:09:15.939 --> 00:09:17.209
Question three.

00:09:17.209 --> 00:09:18.891
What should you do with an approval

00:09:18.891 --> 00:09:20.574
once you're finished using that app?

00:09:24.994 --> 00:09:27.770
The answer: revoke it, or limit the amount to

00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:30.364
exactly what you needed in the first place.

00:09:31.664 --> 00:09:32.919
Question four.

00:09:32.919 --> 00:09:35.748
True or false: signing a plain login message

00:09:35.748 --> 00:09:37.949
can move tokens out of your wallet.

00:09:42.249 --> 00:09:43.884
The answer: false.

00:09:43.884 --> 00:09:45.761
A plain signed message proves

00:09:45.761 --> 00:09:48.064
ownership; it cannot move funds.

00:09:48.064 --> 00:09:51.024
Only an approval combined with a transaction can.

00:09:52.324 --> 00:09:53.639
Question five.

00:09:53.639 --> 00:09:56.974
Priya's wallet shows 'Approve USDC: Unlimited'

00:09:56.974 --> 00:09:59.119
for a $20 swap. What's the

00:09:59.119 --> 00:10:00.434
safer move? [[pause

00:10:04.854 --> 00:10:07.379
4]] The answer: edit the approval down

00:10:07.379 --> 00:10:09.968
to $20, the amount she actually intends

00:10:09.968 --> 00:10:12.299
to swap, before confirming anything.

00:10:13.599 --> 00:10:16.409
Here's the whole lesson, recapped as one loop.

00:10:16.409 --> 00:10:17.784
Connect your wallet.

00:10:17.784 --> 00:10:20.009
Practise first, on a free testnet.

00:10:20.009 --> 00:10:21.536
Read every prompt, and know

00:10:21.536 --> 00:10:23.254
which ones can move funds.

00:10:23.254 --> 00:10:26.024
Limit any approval to what you actually need.

00:10:26.024 --> 00:10:28.204
Transact small and deliberately.

00:10:28.204 --> 00:10:30.449
And revoke the approval once you're done.

00:10:30.449 --> 00:10:33.108
Do those six things, and you're ready for

00:10:33.108 --> 00:10:35.324
real DeFi, at real but tiny stakes.

00:10:36.624 --> 00:10:40.432
This is education, not financial advice, and every dollar

00:10:40.432 --> 00:10:43.949
figure in this lesson is an illustrative example.

00:10:43.949 --> 00:10:46.079
Start on a testnet, then a Layer

00:10:46.079 --> 00:10:48.703
2, then genuinely tiny real amounts.

00:10:48.703 --> 00:10:51.606
And nobody from this program will ever ask for

00:10:51.606 --> 00:10:54.633
your seed phrase, private keys or account access.

00:10:55.933 --> 00:10:59.411
That's your first DeFi steps: the four actions, practice

00:10:59.411 --> 00:11:02.578
mode first, and how an approval actually works.

00:11:02.578 --> 00:11:05.057
Next up, Lesson zero point eight: your

00:11:05.057 --> 00:11:07.853
security baseline, and the language of DeFi.
